<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/06MW">Remark <span data-tag="06MW">8.6.8.8</span> <span class="named">(Right Fibrations)</span>.</a> Let $U: \operatorname{\mathcal{E}}\rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$ be an (essentially small) cartesian fibration of simplicial sets. Then $U$ is a right fibration if and only if its contravariant transport representation $\operatorname{Tr}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{E}}/\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}: \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}^{\operatorname{op}} \rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{QC}}$ factors through the full subcategory $\operatorname{\mathcal{S}}\subset \operatorname{\mathcal{QC}}$. In particular, Proposition <a data-tag="05WF" href="/tag/05WF">8.6.8.3</a> supplies a bijection </p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix@R =50pt@C=50pt{ \textnormal{\{ (Essentially small) right fibrations $U: \operatorname{\mathcal{E}}\rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}$} \}  / \textnormal{Equivalence} \ar [d] \\ \{  \textnormal{Diagrams $\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}^{\operatorname{op}} \rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{S}}$} \}  / \textnormal{Isomorphism}. } \]
</div>
<p> Note that, in this case, $\operatorname{Tr}_{\operatorname{\mathcal{E}}/\operatorname{\mathcal{C}}}$ can be identified with the covariant transport representation for the left fibration $U^{\operatorname{op}}: \operatorname{\mathcal{E}}^{\operatorname{op}} \rightarrow \operatorname{\mathcal{C}}^{\operatorname{op}}$ (since $U$ is a cartesian conjugate of $U^{\operatorname{op}}$, by virtue of Proposition <a data-tag="04CE" href="/tag/04CE">8.6.1.8</a>). </p>
</article>
